  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA warm front lifts north of the area this morning bringing \novernight shower activity to an end. Upper level ridging then \nbuilds in allowing for 925 mb temperatures to climb into the +16\nto +18 C range. How quickly clouds clear behind the front is a \nquestion, but even with halfway decent mixing this should be \ngood for afternoon temperatures at least the mid-70s for much of\nthe area. This is around 20 degrees above normal for this time \nof year so records at climate sites are going to be challenged. \nRecord values for today can be found in the Climate section \nbelow.\n\nClouds and dewpoints increase tonight making for another mild \nnight with temperatures only bottoming out in the mid to upper \n40s in the north, and in the low to mid-50s to the south. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA frontal boundary approaches the northeast on Thursday and \nwill be the focus for wetter weather and potentially a soaking \nrain event for at least a portion of our area as it stalls out. \nThe largest point of uncertainty in the forecast is the speed of\nthe front and where it stalls out as it will be the focus for \nan axis of heavy rain as the trough (and eventually an upper \nlow) draw in tropical moisture and drive PWATs into the 1 to 1.5\ninch range. The global model trends have been toward the front \nstalling just offshore which doesn't rule out a soaking rain, \nbut may keep it confined to the coast. The 04.01Z NBM \nprobabilities of greater than an inch of rain for the Friday \nthrough Saturday timeframe currently sit at 60 to 75 percent at \nthe coast and decrease to 40 to 50 percent up into the \nfoothills. Western New Hampshire and the Western Maine Mountains\nmay miss out on these higher totals, but probabilities of at \nleast half an inch are 40 to 60 percent for these areas. Of \ncourse a slower front and further west stall changes all of \nthis, so trends need to be monitored closely. \n\nLooking ahead into early next week the pattern remains \nunsettled as troughing persists over the northeast. Chances for\nshowers remain in the forecast through Monday, with upslope \nrain and snow showers potentially persisting into Tuesday.",
